WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.233 --> 00:00:01.833
Hugging,

00:00:01.833 --> 00:00:03.700
we are hugging.

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:06.233
I’ve missed the days where my
biggest problem

00:00:06.233 --> 00:00:08.066
was an ever present 
sing-songy twit.

00:00:08.066 --> 00:00:09.466
Drink?

00:00:09.466 --> 00:00:10.133
I’m gonna drink,

00:00:10.133 --> 00:00:12.666
I am not having this conversation
unless I’m drinking.

00:00:12.666 --> 00:00:15.733
I think the most satisfying part of
playing Jaskier in general,

00:00:15.733 --> 00:00:18.933
is the multifaceted nature
with which Lauren has 

00:00:18.933 --> 00:00:21.973
created this character and 
Mr Sapkowski.

00:00:22.300 --> 00:00:28.166
There is so much more to him I think in this season than the silly jokes,

00:00:28.166 --> 00:00:30.400
there’s more than the catchy songs.

00:00:30.400 --> 00:00:33.400
We get to see a more 
well rounded character,

00:00:33.400 --> 00:00:35.133
a textured character

00:00:35.133 --> 00:00:37.966
and somebody who is 
for the first time in his life

00:00:37.966 --> 00:00:42.566
making real moral decisions and
putting his own life at risk.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:43.600
Thank you.

00:00:44.400 --> 00:00:46.100
We… all of us

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.333
would be dead without you.

00:00:47.333 --> 00:00:48.600
Oh.

00:00:48.600 --> 00:00:52.300
It always feels really, really amazing
to approach this character

00:00:52.300 --> 00:00:53.560
every time I return to him,

00:00:53.560 --> 00:00:57.433
because Lauren just keeps adding
more and more layers

00:00:57.433 --> 00:01:01.873
and I’m always so excited to explore
them as the stories progress.

00:01:10.433 --> 00:01:13.633
Joe Trapanese, our amazing
composer, and I,

00:01:13.640 --> 00:01:18.733
worked really hard on the music
throughout the pandemic before shooting

00:01:18.733 --> 00:01:23.833
and it was the first time where I was able
to have a little bit more ownership

00:01:23.833 --> 00:01:25.993
over the songs 
that Jaskier sings.

00:01:26.266 --> 00:01:31.546
♪What for do you yearn?
It’s the point of no return♪

00:01:31.533 --> 00:01:34.533
That meant that by the time we
got to shoot the songs,

00:01:34.533 --> 00:01:36.433
 it really felt very natural

00:01:36.433 --> 00:01:38.866
 I wasn’t worrying about where
the song was going

00:01:38.866 --> 00:01:42.466
on the tonal shifts that
we were aiming to achieve.

00:01:47.200 --> 00:01:49.666
♪Watch that butcher burn♪

00:01:49.666 --> 00:01:52.666
Turning up on set was exhausting

00:01:52.666 --> 00:01:55.226
because we were shooting
all day long

00:01:55.233 --> 00:01:56.666
and a lot of these were one takes

00:01:56.666 --> 00:01:59.300
which just means that we didn’t cut into it

00:01:59.300 --> 00:02:00.966
and that wasn’t as much coverage as usual.

00:02:00.966 --> 00:02:02.900
There was no room to mess up.

00:02:02.900 --> 00:02:04.333
There was a real performative energy to it, 

00:02:04.333 --> 00:02:06.533
it felt a bit like performing a play really.

00:02:06.733 --> 00:02:08.633
Though it looks like we’re filming indoors

00:02:08.633 --> 00:02:10.100
we’re actually exterior.

00:02:10.100 --> 00:02:12.460
It looks like we’re in a tavern but
we’re actually outside

00:02:12.466 --> 00:02:15.566
so you can actually see the cold
in my breath when I’m singing,

00:02:15.566 --> 00:02:19.900
you can actually see all of our
wonderful SA’s, extras,

00:02:19.900 --> 00:02:21.933
shivering in the background,

00:02:21.933 --> 00:02:25.433
and it meant that we all had to
really come together

00:02:25.433 --> 00:02:28.100
and kind of use each other’s
energy throughout the day

00:02:28.100 --> 00:02:32.180
to kind of get something that
didn’t look like we were all 

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:35.240
freezing our cotton socks off
in the middle of winter.

00:02:45.633 --> 00:02:48.300
So we left Jaskier on the top of a mountain

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.380
in episode 6 of Season 1.

00:02:50.900 --> 00:02:52.060
Somehow he got down.

00:02:52.066 --> 00:02:53.826
See you around Geralt.

00:02:53.833 --> 00:02:57.866
We get to see him for the first time
standing on his own two feet

00:02:57.866 --> 00:02:59.986
discovering his own independence

00:03:00.000 --> 00:03:03.720
and discovering who he is when he’s not defined by his friendship with Geralt.

00:03:05.333 --> 00:03:07.066
One of the last scenes that I shot

00:03:07.066 --> 00:03:09.826
was actually in the jail cell with Henry

00:03:09.833 --> 00:03:13.733
and Henry and I got
loads of time to really discuss 

00:03:13.733 --> 00:03:16.466
and work out where to pitch this.

00:03:16.466 --> 00:03:19.866
We didn’t wanna bring in guilt, or shame.

00:03:19.866 --> 00:03:21.986
We wanted to bring in
forgiveness and understanding,

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:25.160
which is what all friendships
should be based on.

00:03:25.160 --> 00:03:27.800
And it meant that by the time

00:03:27.800 --> 00:03:31.300
Henry and I shot that
first scene in the jail cell,

00:03:31.300 --> 00:03:35.500
I think I ad-libbed a line, about 
shoving something up your…

00:03:35.500 --> 00:03:36.733
I can’t remember what it was.

00:03:36.733 --> 00:03:40.433
Sir you would not know talent if
I shoved it up your-

00:03:40.433 --> 00:03:41.900
Geralt.

00:03:41.900 --> 00:03:44.900
And Henry broke in the middle of the first take,

00:03:44.900 --> 00:03:46.533
and it just felt really normal,

00:03:46.533 --> 00:03:50.533
it felt like we were back
and we were in sync again.

00:03:50.533 --> 00:03:51.733
Fuck it.

00:03:52.333 --> 00:03:55.333
There was moments where I get to, you know,

00:03:55.333 --> 00:03:56.566
dance around the scripts,

00:03:56.566 --> 00:04:00.900
but also really enjoy the very natural
chemistry that Henry and I have.

00:04:00.900 --> 00:04:07.300
So I think Jaskier knows that the
rift between them isn’t forever,

00:04:07.300 --> 00:04:11.533
and sometimes time is required
to heal those kinds of wounds.

00:04:11.533 --> 00:04:14.266
You left me on a mountain.

00:04:14.266 --> 00:04:15.786
Have you seen these boots?

00:04:15.780 --> 00:04:18.260
But also he’s not about to

00:04:18.266 --> 00:04:20.900
reignite this friendship easily,

00:04:20.900 --> 00:04:22.220
 there are new terms to it,

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:24.133
there’s a new dynamic

00:04:24.133 --> 00:04:26.600
and he’s perfectly willing to say,

00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:30.500
“Hey Geralt we don’t do this anymore,
we do it like this now.”

00:04:30.500 --> 00:04:32.933
Don’t fucking Jaskier me,
I’m talking to you,

00:04:32.933 --> 00:04:34.033
this is how this works.

00:04:34.033 --> 00:04:35.433
We got to see a stronger Jaskier,

00:04:35.433 --> 00:04:36.900
a Jaskier that is just as kind,

00:04:36.900 --> 00:04:38.233
just as light,

00:04:38.233 --> 00:04:40.993
but with a strength in him now.

00:04:41.000 --> 00:04:44.000
And with a sense of
self perspective, I guess.

00:04:44.266 --> 00:04:46.826
I made new friends, get over it.

00:04:46.833 --> 00:04:47.913
Jealous.

00:04:51.933 --> 00:04:56.600
To be the mouthpiece for Lauren
in these sort of scenes

00:04:56.600 --> 00:04:57.900
is always an absolute joy,

00:04:57.900 --> 00:05:00.260
because Sapkowski does
do this in the books,

00:05:00.266 --> 00:05:02.826
he does get a little
bit metatheatrical,

00:05:02.833 --> 00:05:06.666
he does wink at the reader,
in a very knowing way.

00:05:06.666 --> 00:05:09.186
And the best way that we could
translate that into the show

00:05:09.200 --> 00:05:10.733
was through Lauren’s writing,

00:05:10.733 --> 00:05:13.266
and through Jaskier’s sort of metatheatricality

00:05:13.266 --> 00:05:15.300
within the world anyway,
which we’ve already seen.

00:05:15.300 --> 00:05:18.900
 Here I go again,
just delivering exposition.

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:20.933
Al who was playing the guard on the day,

00:05:20.933 --> 00:05:22.633
we were filming about 4 
o’clock in the morning,

00:05:22.633 --> 00:05:24.666
and we read the scene
together for the first time

00:05:24.666 --> 00:05:25.786
and I started ad-libbing,

00:05:25.800 --> 00:05:29.066
and our wonderful director Sarah Gorman said,

00:05:29.066 --> 00:05:30.533
“Have you guys been practicing this?

00:05:30.533 --> 00:05:32.633
Have you guys been rehearsing
in your trailers?”

00:05:32.633 --> 00:05:34.333
and we said no we’ve just met for the first time,

00:05:34.333 --> 00:05:35.133
but it felt very natural

00:05:35.133 --> 00:05:37.040
and there was a real natural chemistry.

00:05:37.040 --> 00:05:41.733
♪The long necked warriors
with all their tools…♪

00:05:41.733 --> 00:05:42.333
You know it!

00:05:42.333 --> 00:05:43.166
Oh yeah, yeah.

00:05:43.166 --> 00:05:47.833
So I got to really dance around
with some of the insults…

00:05:47.833 --> 00:05:49.433
Am I gonna say it?

00:05:49.433 --> 00:05:50.666
Yeah I’m gonna say it.

00:05:50.666 --> 00:05:53.133
I drew on pretty much every
single Shakespeare play

00:05:53.133 --> 00:05:55.166
that I’ve been in or read.

00:05:55.166 --> 00:05:58.233
A qualling, feculent, a beef witted,

00:05:58.233 --> 00:06:01.266
hell hated, addlepated gooned…

00:06:01.266 --> 00:06:04.666
It meant that we got to
really, really enjoy

00:06:04.666 --> 00:06:09.200
Jaskier’s ego for the first time
in the series I think.

00:06:09.200 --> 00:06:11.066
You know he’s now famous,

00:06:11.066 --> 00:06:12.786
he’s now really well known,

00:06:12.800 --> 00:06:14.600
and he’s using that to help people.

00:06:14.600 --> 00:06:17.200
But he still got that 
recalcitrant side to him,

00:06:17.200 --> 00:06:19.300
where if someone says, I didn’t love it…

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:21.233
It’s not your strongest.

00:06:21.233 --> 00:06:22.533
We got to go…

00:06:22.533 --> 00:06:23.733
Oh yeah…?

00:06:23.733 --> 00:06:26.720
Well then explain why, Twitter!

00:06:26.720 --> 00:06:27.633
It took me to the fourth verse

00:06:27.633 --> 00:06:29.700
to understand they
were different timelines.

00:06:29.700 --> 00:06:30.820
Oh did it? Wow.

00:06:30.833 --> 00:06:32.700
And that magic kiss?
That was a bit cheap.

00:06:32.700 --> 00:06:33.300
Yeah?

00:06:33.300 --> 00:06:36.300
And it’s that open sort of
dialogue that I always think,

00:06:36.300 --> 00:06:38.133
it’s very like Mr Sapkowski,

00:06:38.133 --> 00:06:41.133
but I applaud Lauren for
using this kind of platform

00:06:41.133 --> 00:06:45.253
to enjoy the metatheatricality
of the show itself.

00:06:48.266 --> 00:06:50.386
Once again, we were shooting exterior,

00:06:50.400 --> 00:06:52.400
in spite of the fact it looks interior.

00:06:52.400 --> 00:06:55.666
Filming that scene was one of the most difficult,

00:06:55.666 --> 00:06:58.706
but rewarding experiences
of shooting Season 2.

00:06:59.233 --> 00:07:02.500
I made sure that I stripped myself
of as much artifice,

00:07:02.500 --> 00:07:04.860
and indeed clothing as I possibly could

00:07:04.866 --> 00:07:06.933
so that I could be
in a very cold space,

00:07:06.933 --> 00:07:10.666
with the darkest thoughts that I could 
possibly summon up in my head.

00:07:10.666 --> 00:07:14.800
When a character like that is
confronted with imminent death,

00:07:14.800 --> 00:07:21.066
you’ve got to really find a rawness,
and a natural way of talking.

00:07:21.066 --> 00:07:22.386
He tries to bargain,

00:07:22.400 --> 00:07:24.000
he tries to fight back,

00:07:24.000 --> 00:07:25.233
he goes through all the stages.

00:07:25.233 --> 00:07:26.633
I don’t know where they’re going

00:07:26.633 --> 00:07:30.133
and I don’t know why you’re doing this to me.

00:07:30.133 --> 00:07:31.833
And Chris who plays Rience,

00:07:31.833 --> 00:07:34.300
is one of the most incredibly
intense actors,

00:07:34.300 --> 00:07:38.233
he’s so still, he brings
the sadism to the character,

00:07:38.233 --> 00:07:41.466
the enjoyment of seeing someone else in pain,

00:07:41.466 --> 00:07:43.626
which is so wonderful,

00:07:43.633 --> 00:07:46.833
because he’s an absolute delight off-set.

00:07:46.833 --> 00:07:49.066
He is one of the most wonderful people,

00:07:49.066 --> 00:07:51.500
and yet seeing the shift in him

00:07:51.500 --> 00:07:52.900
did so much work for me.

00:07:52.900 --> 00:07:56.000
I genuinely could
find myself scared,

00:07:56.000 --> 00:07:57.533
and fearful of my life.

00:07:57.533 --> 00:08:02.133
And this is the first time 
Jaskiers not had his best friend

00:08:02.133 --> 00:08:06.233
to come in and swoop in
and save him from danger.

00:08:06.233 --> 00:08:10.313
And so, for the first time
we see him panicking.

00:08:11.500 --> 00:08:12.780
Listen to me please.

00:08:12.800 --> 00:08:15.666
Please listen to me,
I... I am a bard.

00:08:15.666 --> 00:08:17.933
He doesn’t have a backup plan,

00:08:17.933 --> 00:08:19.833
and he can’t talk his way out of it,

00:08:19.833 --> 00:08:24.300
and that helplessness,
stayed with me for weeks

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:25.420
after shooting that scene.

00:08:25.433 --> 00:08:26.633
I felt very very raw

00:08:26.633 --> 00:08:28.500
and emotionally a little
bit vulnerable after it,

00:08:28.500 --> 00:08:32.660
because it really required
a lot of emotional energy

00:08:32.666 --> 00:08:35.546
to try and commit to it as we did.

00:08:39.766 --> 00:08:41.766
The monster mash was grueling,

00:08:41.766 --> 00:08:43.766
it was great fun, but it was grueling,

00:08:43.766 --> 00:08:45.000
it was very very challenging.

00:08:45.000 --> 00:08:46.533
It took us about three and a half weeks,

00:08:46.533 --> 00:08:49.133
maybe a month to shoot that entire sequence.

00:08:49.133 --> 00:08:51.893
They were long days, lots of stunts.

00:08:53.100 --> 00:08:55.333
I spent most of that time under a table,

00:08:55.333 --> 00:08:56.733
for about three and a half weeks,

00:08:56.733 --> 00:09:00.466
and scrambling around, 
and screaming my lungs out,

00:09:00.466 --> 00:09:04.826
with lots of men in green tights
jumping around me

00:09:04.840 --> 00:09:05.700
as the Basilisk,

00:09:05.700 --> 00:09:08.220
so it was a very bizarre experience.

00:09:13.233 --> 00:09:15.700
My first impression was actually ad-libbed

00:09:15.700 --> 00:09:17.420
in that episode I believe.

00:09:17.433 --> 00:09:22.193
I think Freya just walked away
whilst I tried to talk to her,

00:09:22.200 --> 00:09:24.233
and then I said something
along the lines of…

00:09:24.233 --> 00:09:25.753
Like father, like daughter..

00:09:27.233 --> 00:09:32.193
That very much summed up
Jaskier’s outlook towards Ciri.

00:09:32.200 --> 00:09:34.333
My outlook towards
Freya is completely different.

00:09:34.333 --> 00:09:37.566
I’ve so enjoyed working with Freya,

00:09:37.566 --> 00:09:39.966
she’s got such a stillness, and a natural -

00:09:39.966 --> 00:09:44.100
a naturalness to her tone when she’s on screen.

00:09:44.100 --> 00:09:49.333
I often sneak behind the camera and watch her 
takes because I find it so fascinating.

00:09:49.333 --> 00:09:53.666
She’s an absolute joy, and so
ridiculously talented

00:09:53.666 --> 00:09:59.226
that I can’t wait to see their dynamic
progress into Season 3.

00:09:59.233 --> 00:10:01.433
You know there is a sort of kismet,

00:10:01.433 --> 00:10:03.133
there is an understanding between them,

00:10:03.133 --> 00:10:08.233
and there is something of the weird
Uncle Jaskier about the whole set up.

00:10:08.233 --> 00:10:09.993
One big happy family, eh?

00:10:10.000 --> 00:10:11.000
No.

00:10:11.000 --> 00:10:12.666
I can’t wait to have more scenes with her,

00:10:12.666 --> 00:10:15.306
she’s astonishing.

